# Line endings are normalised to LF inside the repository; what lands in the
# working tree is then decided per pattern below.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# With core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) and no attributes, git rewrites
# every text file to CRLF on checkout - including shell scripts. A script whose
# shebang ends in CR fails on Linux with a message that points nowhere useful:
#
#   exec /entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# The kernel is looking for the interpreter "/bin/sh\r", which does not exist.
# backend/entrypoint.sh is copied straight into the runtime image and used as
# its ENTRYPOINT, so a CRLF checkout breaks the container even though the blob
# in git was always LF.

# Let git detect text files and store them with LF.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must keep LF in the working tree too, on every platform: they
# are executed inside Linux containers regardless of where they were checked out.
*.sh text eol=lf
